nges in  various surface markers on these cells  using a whole range of flow cytometry  and we can see changes in the th1 th2  t-reg th17 balance in these pregnant  women and what we see are a lot of  things that are reminiscent of what we  see in non-pregnant women but we've now  confirmed that they're occurring in  pregnant women and again if you're  interested i refer you to that paper  that's just been published in clinical  experimental immunology  so that's thinking about our whole blood  approach now we're thinking about our  mononuclear cells and we're taking them  through using our cd14 microbeads that  selva introduced you to using the  methods you introduced you to and  positively selecting our cd14 positive  mo our cd14 positive monocytes and we  get nice purity we would run at about a  92 to 95 percent purity of the cells uh  most days in my laboratory  and so we can use these for a whole host  of downstream uh analyses and i'm just  going to walk you through a few examples  of those different types of approaches  that we take so the first one is just  you know  i don't want to say straightforward  simple flow cytometry i love flow  cytometry it's a great tool  it's one of the key tools that we use in  my laboratory but we can do lots of  powerful things of course we can do this  in our whole blood and i've already  given you an example and we can do it on  our mononuclear cells but because we're  ultimately interested in potentially  subsetting these based on their cd16  expression we've gone with a  uh  using our purified monocytes for much of  this analysis but again it depends on  the studies we're doing so here we're  just looking at cd16 expression of those  monocytes that we've isolated and here  we're now moving into studies of women  at term pregnancies so these are women  just a few days to a few weeks before  they have their babies uh after 37 weeks  of gestation  and so we can use cd16 uh to stain the  cells and then we can see there are  di