led  us to look at the same cells for their  miter tracker expression and take them  through to mass spectrometry as well to  do the cardio lipid analysis we then  wanted to look at the functional  responses of these cells so the method  the methodological approach we then took  was to do extracellular flux analysis  anyone who is familiar with this might  call it seahorse analysis which confuses  people people are always a bit surprised  when we use the terminology because they  know we're a human immunology lab and  they get very confused when we mention  seahorses and we have to explain it's  just the the  slang name for the piece of equipment  but this particular piece of equipment  allows you to actually measure  oxidative phosphorylation and glycolysis  in intact cells and we do this on our  monocytes as i'm showing you here today  but we do it on t cells and eosinophils  and neutrophils and all kinds of immune  cells  and this enables you to do um to inject  the cells with a whole host of different  inhibitors and activators and uncouplers  of the different like different  metabolic processes within the cell so  you can see the little table on the  bottom left it's got the four key drugs  that i'm going to be using in the assays  i'm talking about today and we can  inject these in different sequences and  then we can measure what's called the  oxygen consumption rate up on the top  right there of the slide or we can  measure the extracellular acidification  rate which is in the bottom right there  of the slide  and the opposite opposite consumption  which you know use the shorthand of  occur allows us all kinds of measures of  oxidative phosphorylation you can use  different points on that that graph  that's or that that trace that is  generated and you can calculate  different things such as spare  respiratory capacity maximum represent  maximal respiration and a whole host of  different parameters and then equally on  the bottom you can do the same thing but  here you're measuring extra cellular  acidification rate which is a measure of  glycolysis and again you can look at  various glycolysis parameters using the  data that you generate in that trace so  we took our monocytes that we purified  on the auto max and we were able to put  them into the plates for the actual  cellular flux analysis  and then we can generate those oxygen  consumption rate traces and those extra  cellular acidification rate traces as  i've given you the example on the  previous slide so that's what's now  shown on this slide again we've taken  monocytes around non-pregnant and  pregnant women at 37 plus weeks such as  the most startling thing you'll see is  the oxidative consumption rate again our  pregnant women are in blue and you can  see they have a very reduced oxidative  consumption rate suggesting they've got  reduced oxidative phosphorylation and  you can see that summarized in the graph  below is the respiration rate calculated  from that trace above and you can see  that maximal respiration rate is  different significantly reduced in our  pregnant women on the right we've got  glycolysis and we can see there's not  any real significant difference in the  glycolysis that's occurring in these  cells and to us of course that made  sense we knew our monocytes had less  mitochondria in them so it was perhaps  not surprising that we looked at  cellular metabolism focusing on  oxidative phosphorylation and glycolysis  it was only oxidative phosphorylation  that showed that decline in our pregnant  women  and then we wanted to see what the  functional consequences of that were  those for those effective function that  the cell is doing so we know we've got  that fewer mitochondria we know that  there's less oxidative phosphorylation  in these cells from our pregnant women  well what's going on in terms of an  effector response by those monocytes and  we use cytokine readout to look at that  but of course you have to be very  thoughtful about what you stimulate your  cells with lots of people would  stimulate their cells with liver  polysaccharide for example which is what  we did and we didn't find much of a  difference the lipid polysaccharide  activity is very dependent on glycolysis  and we knew that glycolysis was not  dampened in our cells from our pregnant  women so we searched around for  something that may be a stimulus that  was dependent on oxidative  phosphorylation and we set it on muramal  dipeptide or mdp which is a nod to nlr  ligand and you can see the cytokine  responses from our pregnant are not  pregnant women on the right and we can  see reduced interleukin 1 beta  significantly reduced interleukin-6 and  tnf alpha in the cells from our pregnant  women indicating that  poor oxidative phosphorylation that  lesser mitochondrial mass is having that  functional effect on the cells  and so we wanted to really get in and  understand why this may be occurring so  we then did transcriptomics on the cell  and we use the nanostring  encounter sprint profiler and on this  you can you can analyze 800 target genes  on your cells you only need about 80 000  cells we chose the encounter metabolic  pathways panel because we're interested  in cellular metabolism and in our case  this was particularly beneficial because  we didn't have to extract rna we could  make cell lysate so meant we could uh  increase the workflow and we could also  ensure that we were getting good  fidelity about of our assays just on  those very small numbers of cells that  you can use there and the little cartoon  on the right there is just the workflow  for the for how the nanostream works  and so then the kinds of readouts you  get there are those things you see in  all the papers on transcriptomics you  can take this volcano plot on the left  here which is gene by gene analysis and  here we're looking you know on the right  of the volcano with things that are  higher in our pregnant women compared to  our not pregnant women and on the left  we've got those things that are lower  you know not pre in our pregnant women  versus them not pregnant women and we  can see there's lots of genes of  interest that have come up and we're  pursuing those at the moment  we can also take a sort of more heat map  approach and we can see that heat map  analysis on the right here we've got our  not pregnant women on the top of the  heat map not pregnant one to six and  then our pregnant women down the bottom  we've got different pathways  along the y-axis of the heat map and a  blue is an increase in expression and  the amber is a decrease in expression  and there's lots of different pathways  in this in this  metabolic pathways panel that are up and  down regulated in our pregnant women  these include amino acid synthesis  mitochondrial respiration and so again  they're helping us understand what's  going on in those pregnant women  so just to summarize that before we get  to just a few examples of the monocyte  derived macrophages and the placental  macrophages  we showed that there was an increase in  the non-classical monocytes in pregnancy  confirming what was already known but we  were the first to show this reduced  mitochondrial content using our flow  cytometry and our mass spectrometry of  our really highly purified monocytes  we've showed reduced oxidative  phosphorylation and that this is  associated with decreased response to a  pathogen-associated molecular pattern as  long as that pamp is dependent on  oxidative phosphorylation and we can see  relevant changes in our metabolic  pathways using that nanostring-based  transcriptomics so immunometabolic  adaptation is definitely occurring in  the monocytes of these pregnant women  and we're now trying to understand why  and sort of the timelines of this during  the pregnancy  and so some of the other things we're  doing as well and i touched on this  earlier in the presentation we've talked  about the monocytes in the blood but i  just want to give you a couple of  examples in the last few slides of  monocyte derived macrophages and our  macrophages from our tissues  so this is the only example of our  monocyte derived macrophages we're  looking at monocyte-derived osteoclasts  in particular and here we're taking out  you know unnicely pure monocytes we then  expose them to that defined cytokine  environment which in this case is mcsf  and rank ligand and we culture the cells  in this for 11 to 14 days and over that  period they very nicely turn into these  highly sensitized osteoclasts the  macrophages that we would find in the  bone for example these are the macros we  would find in the bone and you can see  the confocal microscopy along the bottom  there de naught we can see our beautiful  little monocytes that little  uh the red is the cytoplasm or the sorry  the plasma membrane and the blue is the  nucleus and you can see the nice kidney  shape of our monocyte nucleus and then  over the course of the culture and the  presence of those two cytokines they  start to merge these different monocytes  and they form these multi-nucleated  giant cells which you can see in the  other images there we can see cells with  you know six seven nuclei inside them  and then we're staining them with trap  and trap antibody which is a antibody  for an enzyme that this particular cell  type produces which helps it degrade the  bone then we've got tom 20 which is a  mitochondrial stain so you can see those  green mitochondria clustering around the  nuclei and then we've got the rank  antibody which is the receptor for the  rank ligand we use to differentiate  cells  the very last thing i want to do is talk  about that placenta we get that placenta  it's a natural waste product after the  baby's born it normally gets chucked out  by the clinical team so we we acquire  that and then we take it and we process  it through for macrophages and you can  see the fetal side there on the left  which has got the umbilical cord coming  out which of course will be attached to  what becomes all of our belly buttons  and then we've got the maternal side  down there  and we're taking a very similar approach  that i've got to like i've already  described to you we take our placentas  we take them from women of different  bmis is what i'm talking about today and  we isolate those placental macrophages  and again we use the auto max to do this  and we're taking a very similar strategy  to what i've described with the monocyte  so i'm not going to go into any great  detail we use the seahorse analyzer the  extracellular flux analysis and we can  see the oxidative consumption as a  measure of oxidative phosphorylation we  can see the extracellular acidification  as a measure of glycolysis  and it's a little bit more complicated  than what i showed you with the  monocytes because here we've looked at  the cells after they've been cultured in  either the absence or presence of  lipopolysaccharide and what we found was  those macrophages from our obese women  and here we use women who have a bmi  greater than 35 so they're you know  they're  they're quite obese by this stage and  we're comparing them to lean women and  we can see that there is a dramatic  difference the macrophages from our  obese women have very reduced oxidative  phosphorylation but they also have very  reduced glycolysis so we're now trying  to tease apart what that means  functionally for the cells  i just want to give you one example of  how we can analyze those functionally we  can use a  frodo labeled e-coli to look at the  phagocytosis of these cells here outside  the cell ph  stops the fluorescence of the frodo  labeled e coli but once the e coli is  internalized and the ph of the lysosome  they're exposed to the ph of the  lysosome they will start to fluoresce  and can be detected on the flow  cytometer so we can do lots of lovely  flow cytometry analysis there as well  and then what we found on the right was  that our ob